2026-11-04 — **Handling rule fixed now, before any result is read.** q17 stopped at Step 0's
  UPGRADE branch and asked its user to approve the upgrade plan. That is the protocol's own gate,
  owned by the fixture's user role, which in a harness round is the operator; it is not a sandbox
  denial and nothing is being laundered. 14.8 ran the identical prompt and 3/3 arms passed this
  point, so the gate firing is variance, not a property of either side.
  **Rule: any arm that stops at Step 0 receives one approval message, byte-identical across arms,
  and nothing else.** Text: "Approved. Apply the upgrade plan as written, then carry out the
  task." Recorded here before the recall metric is computed so the handling cannot become an
  uncontrolled variable, which is the 18.2a lesson applied in advance.
  Separately, for Milestone 19: this is the **first arm in this repo's record to stop at that gate
  rather than override it**. 19.1's 6/6 override came from 18.2's arms. One arm is not a rate;
  logged as a data point, not a finding.
→ next: send q17 the approval, then score batch 1 once both arms are complete.

2026-11-04 — **BATCH 1 VOID, not scored.** Neither arm produced the artifact under test, and for
  one shared cause that is mine:
    q17 (C) stopped at Step 0's UPGRADE gate and asked for approval. No scaffold, no code map.
    q16 (S) did **not** stop, but wrote no scaffold either: it reported the gate unreachable,
      skipped the protocol entirely and shipped the feature. No `03-code-map.md`.
  **2/2 arms blocked or derailed by Step 0, 0/2 inventories produced.** 14.8 ran the same fixture
  and the same setup script and got 3/3 code maps, so the difference is the launch prompt. 14.8's
  wrapper was not stored verbatim; the one used here was reconstructed and is evidently not
  equivalent. That is the defect: **a round whose prompt is reconstructed from a summary is not
  the same round.**
  By the 14.3a and 18.2a precedent the partial state is not scored, and here it is worse than
  partial — the metric is recall over a file the arms never wrote.
  The handling rule recorded above is **withdrawn before use**: approving mid-flight would leave
  q16 writing its map *after* shipping code and q17 writing it *before*, which is an uncontrolled
  variable sitting directly on the metric. Rebuilt clean instead, as 18.2b was.
  **Fix, applied to all six arms equally:** the operator's standing approval for Step 0 moves
  into the launch prompt itself, identical across sides, so the gate cannot fire asymmetrically.
  The prompt is stored verbatim this time → `prompt.txt`.
  **For Milestone 19, a third independent data point** and the first from outside 18.2: a
  `.bemyagent/` holding only `settings.json` sent 2/2 arms down a path neither the protocol nor
  the operator wanted, one stopping and one abandoning the protocol altogether. 19.1 recorded
  6/6 overriding the gate; this is the same condition failing in the two other available ways.
→ next: rebuild all six arms clean, store the prompt, relaunch batch 1.

